WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
OK.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.770
Let's get into audience questions for the
week first one.

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:06.520
Hey, David, with everything going on with
Corona virus, can the United States federal

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.320
government forcibly quarantine people and
not allow them to leave their homes?

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.320
As usual?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.800
This is a question that has a number of different
answers.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.070
Part of it, because we have state and local
government.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:22.160
It's also a question that I would prefer we
not actually test the limits of.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.460
Let's not make it necessary to find out if
the federal government can forcibly require

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.980
people to stay in their homes.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.140
I think we're all much better off going along
with what it is that we are doing to try to

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.710
stop the spread of Corona virus.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.150
I know that it sucks.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.390
I want to be going to places.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.579
I want to go to cafes.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:42.300
I want to be hanging out with people.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.300
I get it.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.300
OK.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.030
But we've all got to do our part time to put
up that.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.390
Pull up your big boy pants and do your part.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.840
But now let's explore it realistically.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.480
Sort of lockdowns would be happening at a
state level rather than the federal government

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:57.480
level.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.210
More than likely, again, we can talk about
these scenarios where martial law is declared

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.900
and the government sends the military.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.570
But more realistically, this would be happening
at the state level.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:13.950
So we actually have an example here in Massachusetts
from the Boston Marathon bombing years ago.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:20.440
And the Scott Sillman Silliman, who is emeritus
director of the Center on Law, Ethics and

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:26.470
National Security at Duke Law School, says
lockdowns really are voluntary.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:32.180
The governor says they want people to shelter
in place and shelter in place can be used

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.550
in a pandemic.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.570
It can be used if there's dangerous people
on the loose.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.190
As was the case with the Boston Marathon bombing.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:44.840
And you're essentially saying stay where you
are either to not expose yourself to a violent

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:50.110
criminals out on the street, to not expose
yourself or others to a virus or disease of

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.159
some kind.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:56.940
Shelter in place that is different than a
state of emergency, which then has more sort

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.180
of financial implications in terms of states
being able to mobilize and accept certain

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.770
resources from the federal government.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:11.920
And in either case, you can try to institute
penalties of fines or prison for people who

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.890
violate some of these decrees.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:20.040
More likely, they take place with violations
of curfews and things associated with states

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.290
of emergency than with requests to shelter
in place.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.430
So, again, it really depends on in which state
this happens.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.840
As we already know, there's a huge partisan
divide with coronavirus.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.990
Republicans are far less worried about it.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.349
Republicans are far less likely to be modifying
their behavior as a result.

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:41.240
Republicans three times more likely to be
hanging out in bars and restaurants and ignoring

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.920
all of the recommendations from the CDC.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.200
You would, I would imagine, also see different
states handle this differently.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.069
And as a result, you would likely start to
see some trends in terms of states that are

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:54.720
doing better and worse in terms of the virus.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:05.220
My recommendation is let's not make it necessary
to test the legal limits of recommendations

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.410
that people stay home.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:13.430
Now, from a practical perspective, if you
close down schools, ban gatherings of more

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:20.360
than 25 people, closed down bars and restaurants
other than for takeout and delivery, if banks

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:26.780
close a lot of public facing, businesses close
and you shut down public transportation, which

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.470
can be done more relevant in cities, you are
going to get a lot of people who just aren't

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.310
going to go anywhere because where the hell
are they going to go?

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:38.129
And that's also becoming a reality, certainly,
certainly here in Massachusetts.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:40.069
But let's let's cooperate.

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:41.530
Let's do the right thing.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.670
Let's understand that even if we think when
we go out, we're not doing anything.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:53.340
Imagine that when you go out, you're putting
an 80 year old or a kid who's immunocompromised

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.340
in danger.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:55.340
I don't want to be doing that.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.530
I don't want to participate in that.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:57.420
So I'm limiting what I'm doing.

